      "text": "To determine the number of the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria, we start by noting the pattern in the number of bacteria each day. The colony starts with 3 bacteria and doubles its population each day. Therefore, the number of bacteria at the end of day \\( n \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[ P(n) = 3 \\cdot 2^n \\]\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( P(n) > 100 \\). So, we set up the inequality:\n\\[ 3 \\cdot 2^n > 100 \\]\nFirst, we divide both sides by 3:\n\\[ 2^n > \\frac{100}{3} \\]\n\\[ 2^n > 33.3333 \\]\nNext, we need to find the smallest integer \\( n \\) that satisfies this inequality. We can do this by testing successive values of \\( n \\):\nFor \\( n = 5 \\):\n\\[ 2^5 = 32 \\]\nFor \\( n = 6 \\):\n\\[ 2^6 = 64 \\]\nSince \\( 32 < 33.3333 \\) and \\( 64 > 33.3333 \\), the smallest \\( n \\) that satisfies the inequality is \\( n = 6 \\). Therefore, the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria is day 6.\nThe answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
