      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of days it takes for the colony of bacteria to grow from 3 to more than 100. The colony doubles in size every day, starting with 3 bacteria.\n\nLet's denote the number of bacteria at the end of day \\( n \\) as \\( B(n) \\). According to the problem, the number of bacteria doubles each day. Therefore, we can express the number of bacteria at the end of day \\( n \\) as:\n\\[ B(n) = 3 \\times 2^n \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest integer \\( n \\) such that \\( B(n) > 100 \\). This gives us the inequality:\n\\[ 3 \\times 2^n > 100 \\]\n\nFirst, we divide both sides of the inequality by 3:\n\\[ 2^n > \\frac{100}{3} \\]\n\\[ 2^n > 33.33 \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the smallest integer \\( n \\) that satisfies this inequality. We can do this by calculating successive powers of 2:\n\\[ 2^4 = 16 \\]\n\\[ 2^5 = 32 \\]\n\\[ 2^6 = 64 \\]\n\nWe see that \\( 2^5 = 32 \\) is less than 33.33, but \\( 2^6 = 64 \\) is greater than 33.33. Therefore, the smallest integer \\( n \\) that satisfies the inequality is \\( n = 6 \\).\n\nTo confirm, we calculate the number of bacteria at the end of day 5 and day 6:\n\\[ B(5) = 3 \\times 2^5 = 3 \\times 32 = 96 \\]\n\\[ B(6) = 3 \\times 2^6 = 3 \\times 64 = 192 \\]\n\nSince 192 is greater than 100, the number of the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria is day 6.\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
